README-METAFONT -- rendering a font on this disk================================================Metafont (virmf, inimf) turns a font's source into a bitmap.  gftype,gftopk, pktype, pktogf, vptovf and vftovp read and convert what itwrites.  This file shows how to use them.What ships----------Fonts.  Computer Modern at 300 dots per inch -- the sixteen fonts PlainTeX preloads and others, LaTeX's circle fonts among them.  Each is inSYS/TEX/FONTS/PK300 as a packed .pk and in SYS/TEX/FONTS as a .gf,because the drivers here read one form or the other.  PK300/Makefile isthe recipe for the whole Computer Modern set at any resolution.PK144 is empty.With these, every DVI driver here renders the sample story.  dvialw,dvidjp, dviimp, dvijep and dvilj2 use the 300 dpi fonts as they are;dvieps, dvijet, dvimac, dvioki and dvitos want other resolutions andscale the 300 dpi ones by nearest neighbor.  dvips has its PostScriptprologues in SYS/TEX/DVIPS; a size that has not been built is reported(`Font cmbx10.432pk not found, characters will be left blank') anddvips offers to make it.  `-M' tells it not to.Bases.  Metafont needs a preloaded base the way TeX needs a format.SYS/TEX/MFBASES holds plain.base and cmbase.base, and virmf findsplain.base with nothing set.  cmbase is what the Computer Modern sourcesin MFINPUTS/CM are written against.Building a base---------------inimf builds one from SYS/TEX/MFINPUTS in about a minute.  Put the line    plain; \input modes; dumpin mf.in and run    ksh -c "cd /dd/tmp; inimf < mf.in"That writes plain.base.  Add your local modes the same way.  cmbase is    inimf '&plain cmbase dump'and needs MFINPUTS/CM on the search path, which the compiled-in pathdoes not reach:    export MFINPUTS=.:/dd/SYS/TEX/MFINPUTS/CM:/dd/SYS/TEX/MFINPUTS`ksh -c "cd ..."' because Metafont writes its output into the datadirectory, and an interactive bash's `cd' does not move that.  SeeREADME-SHELLS.Rendering a font----------------logo10.mf is the smallest: the seven letters of the METAFONT logo, withno Computer Modern needed.  Pick a mode out of MFINPUTS/modes.mf;`epsonlo' is a low-resolution one, which keeps the bitmap small.    echo "\\mode=epsonlo; mode_setup; input logo10" > /dd/tmp/run.mf    ksh -c "cd /dd/tmp; virmf < run.mf"Metafont prints the character codes as it renders them and writeslogo10.120gf (the bitmap), logo10.tfm (the metrics TeX reads) andlogo10.log.A Computer Modern font:    virmf '&cmbase' '\scrollmode; \mode:=epsonlo; \input cmr10; \end'renders all 128 characters of cmr10 in seconds and writes cmr10.120gf,cmr10.tfm and cmr10.log.The job name comes from the command line.  The same line fed on standardinput renders the same font and names it `mfput' -- mfput.120gf,mfput.tfm.  That is Metafont as specified.`epsonlo' is not a square-pixel mode, so pktype warns `aspect ratio not1:1!'.  Pick the mode that matches your printer.maketexpk (CMDS/TEXCMDS) runs    virmf '&plain \scrollmode; \mode:=%s; \mag:=%s; \input %s; \end'then files the result with `makdir', `del' and `attr' -- your own OS-9's,which SYS/login makes resident when your system is on /h1.  Without them,run the virmf line yourself and then gftopk; that is all maketexpk does.The search paths do not include the current directory-----------------------------------------------------The paths are compiled into the binaries.  The font ones are    /dd/SYS/TEX/FONTS:/dd/USR/TEX/FONTSwith no leading `.', where the Metafont source path has one.  So gftype,gftopk, pktype, pktogf, vftovp and vptovf answer    logo10.120gf: GF file not foundfor a file in the current directory.  Name the directory in thematching variable:    export GFFONTS=/dd/tmp    export PKFONTS=/dd/tmp    export VFFONTS=/dd/tmp    export TEXFONTS=/dd/tmp:/dd/SYS/TEX/FONTS/TFMThe full set read is GFFONTS, PKFONTS, VFFONTS, TEXFONTS, TEXINPUTS,TEXFORMATS, TEXPOOL, MFINPUTS, MFBASES and MFPOOL.  An output filenameis never searched for, so an absolute output path needs no variable.Reading what Metafont wrote---------------------------    gftype -i logo10.120gfprints the glyphs as asterisks; `-m' adds the opcodes beside them.    gftopk logo10.120gf logo10.120pkpacks it.  pktype prints the packed form, glyphs included, and pktogfunpacks it.  The unpacked file is slightly larger than the original:pktogf writes a longer preamble comment.  The bitmap is unchanged.    tftopl logo10.tfm logo10.plreads the metrics -- what TeX uses; the .gf is for the printer.Virtual fonts-------------A virtual font has no glyphs.  Each character is a short program sayingwhat to set from a real font, so one .vf can gather characters fromseveral fonts, shift them, or draw rules between them.    vptovf /dd/DOC/tex/sample.vpl /dd/tmp/s.vf /dd/tmp/s.tfm    vftovp s.vf s.tfm /dd/tmp/back.vplDOC/tex/sample.vpl maps `A' onto cmr10's `A'.  vftovp reads the pairback to text, filling in cmr10's real checksum where the source hadzero.Rendering for a driver----------------------A .pk is for one resolution.  For a printer the shipped set does notcover, render the fonts the document uses at its resolution.  dvijetasks for 120 dpi for the sample DVI, which is `epsonlo':    virmf '&cmbase' '\scrollmode; \mode:=epsonlo; \input cmr10; \end'    gftopk cmr10.120gf /dd/tmp/cmr10.120pk    export TEXFONTS=/dd/tmp    export FONTLIST=pk    dvijet story.dviRender every font the document uses; the sample wants cmsl10 and cmbx10as well as cmr10, and any not rendered set at zero size.  dvijet says`---using nearest neighbor [... [120 dpi]] instead' even on an exactmatch; that is how it reports finding a font by the second of its twofilename forms.